// A comparator has to answer with a number. It used to be that a predicate -
// the shape every other language's sort asks for - was silently read as 1 or 0,
// so a list came out sorted backwards and nothing said why. Now it is a
// TypeError that shows the two comparators side by side.

// Constant predicates, so the message - which quotes the value it got - reads
// the same whichever pair the sort happens to compare first. The two back ends
// use different sort algorithms; the error is what has to match, not the order
// of the comparisons that reach it.
fn outcome(answer) {
    try {
        if (answer) { println([3, 1, 2].sort(fn(a, b) => true)); }
        if (!answer) { println([3, 1, 2].sort(fn(a, b) => false)); }
        return "no error";
    } catch (error) {
        return str(error);
    }
}

// The number comparator is the contract, and it still works: ascending,
// descending, and a tie broken by a second key.
println([3, 1, 2].sort(fn(a, b) => a - b));
println([3, 1, 2].sort(fn(a, b) => b - a));
println(["bb", "a", "cc"].sort(fn(a, b) => a.length - b.length));
println([1.5, -2.0, 0.0].sort(fn(a, b) => a - b));

// A predicate has to say so, and the message has to say what to write instead.
println(outcome(true));
println(outcome(false));

// The message quotes the value it got, so "returned false" and "returned true"
// are both reachable and neither is mistaken for the other.
